#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128830 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "da47f01b-0db0-586f-9d55-5bd4c952afb6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent to Plane a Level Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "09499f38-a497-5f59-9cb2-0882a5853320" :question/math-academy-id "e-3368" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,1)$." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = k\n$$\n\nat the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x_0,y_0,z_0) =(2,-2,1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}i + f_{y}j + f_{z}k \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})j + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})k \\\\\n&= 2xi + 2yj + 2zk \\\\\n∇f(2,-2, 1) &= 2(2)i + 2(-2)j + 2(1)k \\\\\n&= 4i - 4j + 2k \\\\\n&= ⟨4,-4, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}) \\cdot ⟨x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}, z - z_{0}⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n∇f(2,-2, 1) \\cdot ⟨x - 2, y - (-2), z - 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n⟨4,-4, 2⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 2, y + 2, z - 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n4(x - 2) - 4(y + 2) + 2(z - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n4x - 4y + 2z - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n2x - 2y + z - 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128933 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f4bd05-1f30-576c-a538-a0ac4d533b7a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Plane of a Surface Given Implicitly Using the Gradient Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "9091e2dc-5b05-5ae7-a498-9de0483ec375" :question/math-academy-id "e-6183" :question/problem "Consider the surface $z^3y^2 =x^2-5$. Find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at the point $(-1,2,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{3}y^{2} &= x^{2} - 5 \\\\\nz^{3}y^{2} - x^{2} &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = z^3y^2 - x^2\n$$\n\nour surface can be viewed as the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=-5\n$$\n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = k\n$$\n\nat the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}(x, y, z)i + f_{y}(x, y, z)j + f_{z}(x, y, z)k \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(z^{3}y^{2} - x^{2})i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(z^{3}y^{2} - x^{2})j + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z^{3}y^{2} - x^{2})k \\\\\n&= (-2x)i + 2z^{3}yj + 3z^{2}y^{2}k \\\\\n∇f(-1, 2,-1) &= (-2)(-1)i + 2(-1)^{3}(2)j + 3(-1)^{2}(2)^{2}k \\\\\n&= 2i - 4j + 12k \\\\\n&= ⟨2,-4, 12⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}) \\cdot ⟨x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}, z - z_{0}⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n∇f(-1, 2,-1) \\cdot ⟨x - (-1), y - 2, z - (-1)⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n⟨2,-4, 12⟩ \\cdot ⟨x + 1, y - 2, z + 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n2(x + 1) - 4(y - 2) + 12(z + 1) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - 4y + 12z + 22 &= 0 \\\\\nx - 2y + 6z + 11 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f4bd05-1f30-576c-a538-a0ac4d533b7a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Plane of a Surface Given Implicitly Using the Gradient Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad834b7c-49e9-59be-8205-07a17a4dce29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127095} {:answer/id #uuid "0c20ebcd-642f-5240-9390-42734ce34ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127096} {:answer/id #uuid "660524c0-2ac8-5efe-ab65-10f6dfd467d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127097} {:answer/id #uuid "6804a952-ced6-515d-961b-5203f9477d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127098} {:answer/id #uuid "68060bc0-9051-51b0-93a9-20bc0bac59ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c20ebcd-642f-5240-9390-42734ce34ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127096} :answer-field/id #uuid "457ad52a-b7ab-5b66-a472-ff7ca7600097" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127094}] :question/id #uuid "6743007e-663f-5413-8cfe-af47a6a07879" :question/math-academy-id "q-37602" :question/problem "Consider the surface $\\ln (2x^{3} + yz) = 3$. Given that $n = ⟨a, b, c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(e, e^{2},-e)$, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?"}]}
